\title{A Rigorous Census Boundary for $L$-Functions:\\Faithful Arithmetic Coding, Finite-Prefix Ambiguity, and the Dirichlet Model}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{18 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
The proposal that all natural $L$-functions form a countable universe requires care: discrete invariants and finitely many Euler factors do not automatically determine an analytic function. We separate the coding principle that would prove countability from the rigidity statement needed to apply it. Every family admitting an injective code by finite rational arithmetic packets is countable. Conversely, for every finite cutoff, two normalized coefficient sequences bounded by $1$ agree through that cutoff but define distinct convergent Dirichlet series; hence there is no universal finite-prefix classifier. For each positive modulus, distinct complex Dirichlet characters define distinct $L$-functions and the resulting family is finite; over all moduli, analytic Dirichlet $L$-functions form a countable family. For coprime moduli, the character census is multiplicative by Chinese-remainder factorization. These results identify the missing bridge in a census of the Selberg class: a faithful countable code encoding a global rule, rather than finite sampling.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

An $L$-function converts arithmetic data into complex analysis. Its Dirichlet coefficients may describe a character, a modular form, an elliptic curve, or a representation; its Euler factors organize prime-by-prime information; and its functional equation expresses a global symmetry. This unity motivates a basic question: is the collection of natural $L$-functions countable?

Since degrees, conductors, signs, and algebraic coefficients are discrete, an affirmative answer may appear automatic. It is not. A finite amount of metadata is a countable label, but labels prove countability only when they faithfully determine the objects. An analytic function given by a Dirichlet series generally depends on its entire infinite coefficient sequence.

We draw a rigorous census boundary. Faithful coding into a countable packet space gives countability. Dirichlet $L$-functions realize this principle unconditionally: at fixed modulus they form a finite faithfully indexed family, and their union over all moduli is countable. In contrast, no finite coefficient prefix classifies all bounded convergent Dirichlet series.

The key distinction is between \emph{finite samples} and \emph{finite instructions}. A sample records coefficients and leaves the rest unspecified. An instruction, such as a character table together with periodicity and multiplicativity, generates all coefficients. The former cannot support a general census; the latter can, once faithfulness is proved.

\section{Definitions and census principles}

\begin{definition}[Dirichlet series]
A Dirichlet coefficient sequence is a function $a:\mathbb N_{>0}\to\mathbb C$. Its Dirichlet series is
\[
L_a(s)=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}a(n)n^{-s},
\]
for complex $s$ in a region of convergence. We may extend $a$ to index $0$ with the normalization $a(0)=0$. The sequence is \emph{unit bounded} if $|a(n)|\leq 1$ for every $n\geq1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Finite observational agreement]
For $N\in\mathbb N$, sequences $a,b:\mathbb N\to\mathbb C$ \emph{agree through $N$} if $a(n)=b(n)$ for every $0\leq n\leq N$.
\end{definition}

The Selberg class is an axiomatic model for natural $L$-functions: its members possess a normalized Dirichlet series, controlled analytic continuation, a functional equation with gamma factors and conductor, an Euler product, and a Ramanujan-type coefficient bound. We do not assume that the standard metadata and finitely many Euler factors determine a member. That is precisely the rigidity statement at issue.

\begin{definition}[Finite rational arithmetic packet]
A finite rational arithmetic packet consists of a degree $d\in\mathbb N$, a conductor $Q\in\mathbb N$, a root sign $\varepsilon\in\{-1,+1\}$, a finite ordered list of rational gamma shifts, and a finite ordered list of exceptional local factors, each represented by a natural-number index and a finite list of rational coefficients.
\end{definition}

The restriction to discrete data is essential. Allowing an unrestricted complex parameter would destroy the immediate countability argument. A packet assignment $c:\mathcal F\to\mathcal P$ is \emph{faithful} if it is injective.

\begin{definition}[Dirichlet character]
For a positive integer $q$, a complex Dirichlet character modulo $q$ is a periodic, completely multiplicative function $\chi:\mathbb Z\to\mathbb C$ that vanishes away from integers coprime to $q$ and restricts to a homomorphism $(\mathbb Z/q\mathbb Z)^\times\to\mathbb C^\times$. Its $L$-function is
\[
L(s,\chi)=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{\chi(n)}{n^s},
\qquad \operatorname{Re}(s)>1.
\]
\end{definition}

\section{Finite prefixes do not classify}

\begin{theorem}[Finite-Prefix Ambiguity]
For every $N\in\mathbb N$, there exist $a,b:\mathbb N\to\mathbb C$ such that $a(0)=b(0)=0$, both sequences are unit bounded, they agree through $N$, and their Dirichlet series are distinct analytic functions.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Set
\[
a(n)=\mathbf 1_{\{N+1\}}(n),\qquad
b(n)=\mathbf 1_{\{N+2\}}(n).
\]
Both vanish from $0$ through $N$, and all coefficients belong to $\{0,1\}$. Their finite-support series are
\[
L_a(s)=(N+1)^{-s},\qquad L_b(s)=(N+2)^{-s}.
\]
At $s=1$ these take the unequal values $1/(N+1)$ and $1/(N+2)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No Universal Finite-Prefix Classifier]
There is no $N\in\mathbb N$ such that agreement through $N$ forces equality of Dirichlet series for all normalized unit-bounded coefficient sequences.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If such an $N$ existed, the two sequences supplied by the preceding theorem at that cutoff would be required to define equal series, contradicting their explicit values at $s=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Uniqueness of Dirichlet series recovers the full coefficient sequence from the analytic function under standard convergence hypotheses. It does not say that one fixed finite prefix determines the sequence. The same warning applies to finitely many Euler factors unless a special arithmetic rigidity principle controls all omitted factors.
\end{remark}

\section{Faithful packet coding}

\begin{lemma}[Countability of packet space]
The set $\mathcal P$ of finite rational arithmetic packets is countable.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Degrees and conductors lie in $\mathbb N$, the sign lies in a finite set, and shifts are finite lists over $\mathbb Q$. Exceptional factors are finite lists over $\mathbb N\times\mathbb Q^{<\omega}$. Finite lists over countable sets and finite products of countable sets are countable.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Faithful Arithmetic-Packet Census]
If a family $\mathcal F$ admits a faithful assignment $c:\mathcal F\to\mathcal P$, then $\mathcal F$ is countable.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Choose an injection $e:\mathcal P\to\mathbb N$. Then $e\circ c:\mathcal F\to\mathbb N$ is injective.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Conditional Selberg Census]
If every member of the Selberg class has a unique finite rational arithmetic packet, or more generally an injective finite description over a countable alphabet, then the Selberg class is countable.
\end{corollary}

The hypothesis is substantive. The packet may include a finite effective rule generating all omitted local factors, but one must prove that every function has such a rule and that the rule determines it uniquely. A list that merely stops after finitely many factors does not suffice.

\section{The unconditional Dirichlet census}

\begin{lemma}[Fixed-modulus finiteness]
For every positive integer $q$, the set of complex Dirichlet characters modulo $q$ is finite, with cardinality
\[
C(q)=\left|(\mathbb Z/q\mathbb Z)^\times\right|=\varphi(q).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
A character is determined by its restriction to the finite unit group. The complex character group of a finite abelian group is finite and has the same cardinality as the group.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Fixed-modulus faithfulness]
If $\chi$ and $\psi$ are characters modulo the same positive integer $q$ and $L(s,\chi)=L(s,\psi)$ as analytic Dirichlet series, then $\chi=\psi$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Uniqueness of Dirichlet series gives $\chi(n)=\psi(n)$ for every positive integer $n$. Periodicity gives equality on all integers.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Fixed-Modulus Faithful and Finite Census]
For every positive integer $q$, the map $\chi\mapsto L(s,\chi)$ is injective and has finite image. The image contains exactly $\varphi(q)$ distinct functions.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Injectivity is the preceding lemma; finiteness and the cardinality follow from fixed-modulus finiteness.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Global Dirichlet Census]
The family of analytic Dirichlet $L$-functions over all positive moduli is countable.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For each $q\geq1$, the fixed-modulus family is finite. Their union over the countable set of positive integers is countable. Possible duplicate presentations can only decrease the cardinality of the union.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Multiplicative Character Census]
If $m,k$ are coprime positive integers, then
\[
C(mk)=C(m)C(k).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The Chinese remainder theorem gives
\[
(\mathbb Z/mk\mathbb Z)^\times\cong
(\mathbb Z/m\mathbb Z)^\times\times(\mathbb Z/k\mathbb Z)^\times.
\]
A character of a direct product is uniquely a product of characters on the two factors. Taking cardinalities yields the formula, equivalently the multiplicativity of Euler's totient.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
Since $5$ and $8$ are coprime and $C(5)=C(8)=4$, one obtains $C(40)=16$. Coprimality is necessary for the theorem: $C(4)C(2)=2$, whereas $C(8)=4$.
\end{example}

\section{Algorithms and demonstrations}

\subsection{Adversarial prefix construction}
Given any cutoff $N$, store spikes at $N+1$ and $N+2$. A sparse representation uses constant space and evaluates either series in constant time. A dense display through $N+2$ takes $O(N)$ time and space. The exact discrepancy at real $s>0$ is
\[
(N+1)^{-s}-(N+2)^{-s}.
\]
This turns any proposed finite observation horizon into an explicit counterexample.

\subsection{Fixed-modulus census}
The size of the faithfully indexed family modulo $q$ is $\varphi(q)$. Directly counting residues coprime to $q$ uses $O(q)$ gcd calls. Alternatively, after trial division one evaluates
\[
\varphi(q)=q\prod_{p\mid q}\left(1-\frac1p\right)
\]
in $O(\sqrt q)$ arithmetic steps and constant auxiliary space.

\subsection{Multiplicativity audit}
Given $m,k$, compute $\gcd(m,k)$. If it is not $1$, the multiplicative theorem is inapplicable. Otherwise compute $\varphi(m)$, $\varphi(k)$, and $\varphi(mk)$ and compare the last quantity with the product of the first two. With trial-division totients, this takes $O(\sqrt m+\sqrt k+\sqrt{mk})$ arithmetic steps.

\section{Applications and limitations}

A conductor-ordered database requires a canonical representation or equality test, not merely stored metadata. The finite-prefix impossibility theorem rules out one universal prefix test for arbitrary bounded Dirichlet series. Restricted arithmetic families may admit effective tests, but those tests must arise from additional rigidity.

The field of definition also matters. Complex elliptic curves have arbitrary complex $j$-invariants and form an uncountable geometric family. Elliptic curves over $\mathbb Q$, however, are represented by finite tuples of rational coefficients, hence form a countable family. Their arithmetic $L$-functions are therefore at most countable. Arbitrary complex $j$-invariants do not automatically produce arithmetic $L$-functions over $\mathbb Q$.

A proposed conductor-ordered list of the first hundred Selberg-class members would require finite bounded-conductor fibres, a complete candidate generator, effective equality testing, a primitive convention, and a deterministic tie-breaker. These do not follow from packet countability. Countability itself is weaker than finiteness of conductor slices, and polynomial conductor growth is stronger still.

Strong multiplicity-one principles in automorphic families are compatible with our obstruction. They exploit special arithmetic structure absent from arbitrary bounded Dirichlet series. Under bounded degree, conductor, and height, an effective finite prime bound might permit equality testing; the family restrictions are indispensable.

\section{Future research}

Five directions emerge. First, seek faithful arithmetic coding of the Selberg class in which a finite packet encodes an effective global rule for omitted Euler factors. Second, establish finite fibres for primitive functions at fixed degree and bounded conductor. Third, seek polynomial conductor growth in fixed degree after specifying an equivalence relation. Fourth, prove effective rigidity from sparse prime data within bounded automorphic families. Fifth, once finite fibres and equality tests exist, construct a canonical enumeration ordered by conductor and explicit arithmetic tie-breakers.

\section{Conclusion}

Every finite observation horizon misses two bounded coefficient sequences that define distinct convergent Dirichlet series. Finite prefixes therefore do not classify in general. The valid route to a census is instead to construct a countable code and prove it faithful.

Dirichlet $L$-functions demonstrate this route: fixed-modulus families are finite and faithfully indexed, their union is countable, and their counts multiply across coprime moduli. For the Selberg class, countability remains conditional until a suitable global coding or rigidity theorem is supplied. The missing bridge is arithmetic recovery of all coefficients from finite instructions, not analytic uniqueness from the complete sequence.

\end{document}

